ren renames multiple files in a directory according to a search pattern
with wildcards and a replacement pattern, a la VMS (but better).  It
checks for replacement name collisions and handles rename chains and
cycles gracefully.

For example, to change the extension of every .txt file to .bak:

    ren '*.txt' '#1.bak'

See ren.1 for the full description.

Building
========

From a release tarball:

    ./configure && make && make install

From a git checkout, first generate the build system (needs autoconf,
automake and gettext):

    ./autogen.sh

and then proceed as above.

Translating
===========

Messages are translated with GNU gettext.  The template is
po/ren.pot (make -C po ren.pot-update regenerates it).  Translations are
coordinated through the Translation Project, https://translationproject.org/.

Bugs
====

Please report bugs to westes575@gmail.com.

History
=======

ren was written by Vladimir Lanin <lanin@csd2.nyu.edu> and submitted to
comp.sources.unix (Volume 18, Issue 22) in 1988.  This is the author's
comment from that posting:

     This must have been done already by someone somewhere, but I could
     not find it, so here goes.

     Whereas mv can rename (as opposed to move) only one file at a time,
     the following program (ren) can rename many files according to
     search and replacement patterns, ala VMS (but better).  ren checks
     for replacement name collisions and handles rename chains
     gracefully.

     Vladimir Lanin

Rob Schipper <rvs@gol.com> ported it to Linux in 1997.  Will Estes
<westes575@gmail.com> converted it to ANSI C, added autotools and
gettext support, and licensed the result under the GNU General Public
License, version 3 or (at your option) any later version.  The text of
the license is in COPYING.  The original posting carried no license
notice.
